  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nWith regards to print rendering, the specification uses several different words to indicate requirement levels.\n\nThe [printing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#printing) say (emphasis added):\n\n> Even if the user agent doesn't wait at this point, the user agent **must** use the state of the relevant documents as they are at this point in the algorithm if and when it eventually creates the alternate form.\n\nThis seems to apply to *all* printing, including that requested by the user (emphasis added):\n\n> User agents **should** also run the [printing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#printing-steps) whenever the user asks for the opportunity to [obtain a physical form](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#obtain-a-physical-form) (e.g. printed copy), or the representation of a physical form (e.g. PDF copy), of a document.\n\nThe rendering section says (emphasis added):\n\n> When the user actually [obtains a physical form](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#obtain-a-physical-form) (or a representation of a physical form) of a <code>[Document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#document)</code>, the user agent is **expected** to create a new rendering of the <code>[Document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#document)</code> for the print media.\n\nIt isn&#8217;t clear what&#8217;s going on here, but it seems like a kind of contradiction, and it seems like the user is not being put first.\n\nAlso see my comment at <https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11637#issuecomment-3270081865>.",
